# GitHub smart answer

Keep branching rules in reviewed, versioned source, and show the inputs and assumptions behind every answer. Use this variant only for decisions that need complex rules or calculations. Shared decision-aid rules are on [Smart answer](../smart-answer.html), and shared limits are on [Page templates](../index.html).

## When to use it

Use it if a future household diagnostic guide needs branching rules. Keep those rules in reviewed source, with an example for every outcome. Separate logic from explanatory copy. A deterministic answer should cite the inputs and assumptions that produced it ([WG-043](../../../content/writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-043)).

## Structure and behaviour

Define input types, units, unknown states and boundaries before you render questions. Test every branch, going back and changed answers. A calculation must keep the meaning of the underlying measurement and report any approximation. A design template is not authority to invent a formula.

## Home Assistant boundary

civica does not install the government's Ruby smart-answer engine or add a diagnostic calculator. The examples are decision documentation, with no service calls and no inferred fault diagnosis ([WG-056](../../../content/writing-guidelines/summaries.html#wg-056)). Use a simpler written guide unless the extra logic demonstrably helps.

## Decision rule record

A readable rule specification, not an unimplemented calculator.

